Algorithms, including greedy algorithms, divided and conquer, dynamic programming, network flow and 
some complexity theory. 
 
Course Description 
 
Algorithms are the heart of computer science. It provides mathematically rigorous solutions to 
computational problems. This course introduces basic methods for the design and analysis of algorithms. It 
includes greedy algorithms, divide and conquer, dynamic programming and maximum-flow problems. In the 
last part of this course, we will also discuss topics in the theory of computing. Materials such as 
decidability/undecidability, polynomial-time reductions, P v.s. NP, and NP-completeness will be covered. 
 
Learning Objectives 
 
The course is designed to enable students to: 
1. Understand basis algorithms such as greedy algorithms, divide and conquer, dynamic programming 
and graph problems. 
2. Learn how to model computational problems as mathematical problems, and how to analyze 
algorithms rigorously. 
3. Understand basic concepts from the computation theory such as P and NP.
